# Restaurant print relay — do not regress

- **Pipeline, not batch barrier.** Each claimed job is acked/released as soon as it finishes (`runJob`); never `Promise.allSettled` the whole claim batch before acking or claiming more — one slow printer would stall every station.
- **Cap in-flight, scaled by printer count** (`maxInFlight(caps)` = printers × `MAX_LANE` + 4, min 24) and send `limit` in the claim request. The stale-claim safety comes from the **per-lane** cap, not the global one; a fixed global 24 left 20 printers idle ~30% of the time (34 of 50 slips/s). After a full batch claim again immediately, do not wait for the next poll. Skip claimed ids already in flight (stale reclaim) to avoid double print.
- **Partial index `idx_rst_print_jobs_pending_printer`** (v40) must use exactly the claim expression `lower(coalesce(payload::json->>'printerName', ''))` `WHERE status = 'PENDING'`. If the claim SQL expression changes, change the index too — without it claim p95 grew from ~9 ms to ~350 ms under 2 000 inserts/s.
- **Fair share per printer, not FIFO across all.** Claim SQL takes the oldest `perLane` category slips **per printer** (`unnest(printers) CROSS JOIN LATERAL … LIMIT perLane FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`) — no global scan window, which a deep backlog on one printer would fill — and skips `busyPrinters` (local lane ≥ `BUSY_AT` or in `PRINTER_COOLDOWN_MS` after an error). Never go back to plain `ORDER BY created_at LIMIT n` with one global cap — a flooded or dead printer would take every slot and the other stations wait. Keep `BUSY_AT - 1 + PER_PRINTER_CLAIM` × job timeout under `CLAIM_STALE_SECONDS`. Load tests: `restaurant-frontend/test/restaurant/pos/hooks/printRelayLoad.test.js` (relay sim, 10 and 20 printers, 2 000 slips/s) and `PrintJobClaimSqlLoadTest` (real SQL on Postgres) — keep them green.
- **Never drop an ack.** A failed ack/release goes back to its queue and is retried (`SETTLE_RETRY_MS`); jobs printed on this laptop are remembered (`printedAt`, 20 min) and, if the server re-hands them after the 45 s stale reclaim, are acked without printing. `printedAt` is persisted in localStorage so an app restart does not reprint either. Venue peak scenario: `restaurant-frontend/test/restaurant/pos/hooks/printPeakScenario.test.js`.
- **Unsure → do not reprint.** A print timeout / worker exit mid-job (`isUncertainPrintError`) is acked with mode `uncertain` + a toast to the cashier, never released: the slip may already be in the Windows queue. Only errors before the spooler took the data are released. Keep `return await` inside the `try` in `executeRestaurantPrintJob`, otherwise errors bypass this split.
- Autoprint categories whose `printPrinterName` is not a Windows printer on this PC get one toast per name (checked every 60 s by the leader) — no silent slip loss.
- `printRelayLeader.start()` must stay idempotent (one heartbeat interval); a leaked interval holds the lock after `stop()` and the next relay instance never prints.
- **One slip at a time per printer** (`runOnPrinterLane` in `printTicketLines`); different printers stay parallel. The print timeout starts after the lane, not while waiting.
- **Windows RAW goes through a long-lived PowerShell worker per printer** (`win-raw-print-worker.ps1`, one pre-warmed spare). Do not go back to spawning `powershell.exe` + `Add-Type` per slip (1.5–4 s each). The one-shot path is only a fallback when the worker cannot start (nothing sent yet), never after a job was written to the worker, because that would print twice.
- **Windows RAW temp file must be unique per job** (`randomUUID`), never `Date.now()` — parallel jobs in the same ms overwrite/unlink each other.
